tion: Multitasking processing deficits revealed by event-related potentials.</article-title><source>Psychophysiology</source><volume>44</volume><fpage>323</fpage><lpage>333</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17343714</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003967-Jolicur1"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jolic&#x00153;ur</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Brisson</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Robitaille</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><year>2008</year><article-title>Dissociation of the N2pc and sustained posterior contralateral negativity in a choice response task.</article-title><source>Brain Res</source><volume>1215C</volume><fpage>160</fpage><lpage>172</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003967-Jolicur2"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jolic&#x00153;ur</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Sessa</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Dell'Acqua</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Robitaille</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>On the control of visual spatial attention: Evidence from human electrophysiology.</article-title><source>Psychol Res</source><volume>70</volume><fpage>414</fpage><lpage>424</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16184394</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003967-Jolicur3"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jolic&#x00153;ur</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Sessa</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Dell'Acqua</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Robitaille</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Attentional control and capture in the attentional blink paradigm: Evidence from human electrophysiology.</article-title><source>Eur J Cogn Psychol</source><volume>18</volume><fpage>560</fpage><lpage>578</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003967-Klaver1"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Klaver</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Talsa</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Wijers</surname><given-names>AA</given-names></name><name><surname>Heinze</surname><given-names>HJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Mulder</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>An event-related brain potential correlate of visual short-term memory.</article-title><source>Neuroreport</source><volume>10</volume><fpage>2001</fpage><lpage>2005</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10424664</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003967-McCollough1"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>McCollough</surname><given-names>AW</given-names></name><name><surname>Machizawa</surname><given-names>MG</given-names></name><name><surname>Vogel</surname><given-names>EK</given-names></name></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Electrophysiological measures of maintaining representations in visual working memory.</article-title><source>Cortex</source><volume>43</volume><fpage>77</fpage><lpage>94</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17334209</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0003967-Vogel1"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Vogel</surname><given-names>EK</given-names></name><name><surname>Machizawa</surname><given-names>MG</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Neural activity predicts individual differences in visual working memory capacity.</article-title><source>Nature</source><vol